<section class="hero">
      <h1>How to calculate LLM API costs</h1>
      <p class="tagline">Input tokens · output tokens · cache · monthly usage</p>
    </section>

    <p class="lede">
      The <a href="../../cost/">cost calculator</a> gives you the answer directly; this guide explains the principles,
      steps and common mistakes behind it, so you can recompute any vendor's price list yourself. All worked examples
      use Reflex Hub's single data source (models.json) with official sources and verification dates you can check
      step by step. The complete rules for how we compile and present pricing data are in the
      <a href="../../pricing-methodology/">pricing methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>1. What determines API cost</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>Mainstream LLM APIs bill by <strong>token usage</strong>. A monthly bill decomposes into four terms:</p>
        <p>
          · <strong>Call count</strong>: how many requests you make per day;<br>
          · <strong>Input tokens</strong>: the content sent per request (system prompt, conversation history and the user's input all count);<br>
          · <strong>Output tokens</strong>: the content the model generates per request;<br>
          · <strong>Unit prices</strong>: input and output are billed separately per 1M tokens at different rates; some vendors add a cache-hit price and peak/off-peak rates.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Scope</strong>: this article covers pay-as-you-go chat/reasoning APIs billed by tokens. Subscription bundles, per-call billing (some image/video endpoints) and reserved capacity follow their own billing documents.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>2. Price units and multi-currency handling</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>Prices are quoted <strong>per 1M tokens</strong>, consistent with official vendor pricing pages. A token is the smallest unit of text a model processes: roughly 1–2 tokens per Chinese character and about 1 token per English word, depending on each vendor's tokenizer. Mind the conversion: vendors quote "price per 1M tokens", while a single request is usually far below a million — divide by 1,000,000 first.</p>
        <p><strong>Original currencies are preserved</strong>: Chinese vendors (DeepSeek, GLM, Kimi, Qwen, Hunyuan, MiniMax, MiMo, Step) list prices in CNY; OpenAI / Anthropic / Google models are listed in USD. For comparison and estimation, USD prices are converted to CNY at a <strong>reference rate</strong> (the CFETS central parity rate, with the rate date shown) — indicative only. This site never disguises a $ list price as ¥ and never compares raw numbers across currencies.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>3. The formula</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>Compute input and output <strong>separately, then add</strong>:</p>
        <p>
          · Input cost = calls × input tokens per call ÷ 1,000,000 × input price<br>
          · Output cost = calls × output tokens per call ÷ 1,000,000 × output price<br>
          · Total cost = input cost + output cost
        </p>
        <p>The input price may not be a single value: the portion that hits context cache is billed at the lower <strong>cache-hit price</strong>, so:</p>
        <p>· Effective input price = hit rate × cache-hit price + (1 − hit rate) × cache-miss price</p>
        <p>For time-based priced models (e.g. DeepSeek), first determine whether the request lands in a peak or off-peak window — the unit prices differ. Models without a published cache price have no cache-hit price to apply and must be billed at the miss price — unknown is not zero.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>4. Context-length tiers</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>Some models bill by <strong>per-call input length</strong>: MiniMax M3, for example, charges the lower tier for inputs ≤512K (input ¥2.1 / output ¥8.4 per 1M tokens) and jumps to the higher tier above 512K (¥4.2 / ¥16.8); Claude Haiku 5.5 splits at a 100K prompt length into $0.1 / $0.5 tiers. Pick the tier from your per-call input length first, then apply the formula.</p>
        <p><strong>Unpublished tiers are never back-filled</strong>: the &gt;272K long-context tier rates for GPT-6 Luna and GPT-6.1 Sol are not published on the official pricing pages — estimates in that range must honestly return "cannot estimate"; borrowing the lower tier's price would produce a falsely low number. This site's calculator and comparison pages refuse to estimate unpublished tiers.</p>
        <p>In common businesses where a single input stays below the lower-tier ceiling (most chat, RAG and agent workloads are far below 100K–512K), tiers do not affect the result; only whole-document injection needs a tier-boundary check first.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>5. A reproducible worked example</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>The table below walks through every step with a real model whose data is complete (including a published cache price). All values come from this site's single data source models.json; the model page carries the official source and verification date:</p>
      </div>
      
