      <p class="s-hint">To verify: substitute the table's values into the section-3 formula by hand, or open the <a href="../../cost/">cost calculator</a>, select the same model and use the same parameters (100 calls/day · 2,000 input · 800 output · 30% hit rate) to compare results.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>6. Cache read-write and peak/off-peak prices</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p><strong>Cache-hit price</strong>: when a request shares a prefix with previous requests, vendors allow it to hit the context cache, and the hit portion is billed at a cache price well below the standard input price. Keeping a stable system prompt and document prefix raises the hit rate. Only models with a published cache price get this discount; this site bills unpublished models at the miss price and never assumes a cache discount.</p>
        <p><strong>Cache write fees</strong> are an easily overlooked charge: some vendors bill cache writes separately (e.g. GPT-6 Luna's 30-minute tier at $0.125/1M tokens, Anthropic's 5-minute tier from $0.125). Write fees couple with retention duration and usage assumptions, so example monthly costs on this site exclude them; each model profile notes them separately.</p>
        <p><strong>Peak/off-peak pricing</strong>: take DeepSeek as an example — peak hours (weekdays 9:00–12:00 and 14:00–18:00, Beijing time) bill at standard rates, while all other times (nights, lunch hours, weekends and public holidays) bill at about half. Scheduling deferrable batch jobs into off-peak windows is a deterministic cost reduction. This site's calculator and comparison pages compute example monthly costs at the off-peak basis by default and say so on the page.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>7. From per-call cost to monthly estimates</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>Monthly estimate = daily calls × 30 days × per-call cost. Three assumptions need your real business numbers:</p>
        <p>
          · <strong>Call count</strong>: use average daily requests from logs, not peaks;<br>
          · <strong>Input per call</strong>: count the system prompt, conversation history and the current input together — input grows with turns in multi-turn chats;<br>
          · <strong>Output per call</strong>: long-form generation can produce far more output than input, and output is usually priced higher.
        </p>
        <p>Example monthly costs on this site's comparison pages fix 100 calls/day, 2,000 input / 800 output tokens per call, off-peak rates and a 30% cache hit rate. They exist for horizontal comparison only — they are not your actual bill.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>8. Common mistakes</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>
          · <strong>Mistake 1: input and output cost the same</strong>. Output is usually 2–4× the input price; pricing by input alone severely underestimates;<br>
          · <strong>Mistake 2: treating unknown prices as zero</strong>. An unpublished cache price does not mean caching is free — pricing it at zero fabricates a low cost;<br>
          · <strong>Mistake 3: comparing raw numbers across vendors</strong>. Tier boundaries and discount strategies differ; normalize first — list prices, same tier, same usage assumptions;<br>
          · <strong>Mistake 4: ignoring the output share</strong>. Summarization and writing workloads are often output-dominated — raise the output weight in your estimate;<br>
          · <strong>Mistake 5: treating an estimate as an invoice</strong>. Free tiers, resource packs and tiered pricing can all push the real bill below the list-price estimate — or above it once free quotas run out. Estimates are for budgeting and comparison only.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>9. Data sources and verification</h2>
      <div style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">
        <p>
          Reflex Hub maintains model prices and specs in a single data source, models.json; every record carries an official source link and a "last verified" date (verifiedAt), and the footer aggregates all sources. <strong>Price-verification time ≠ page-content update time</strong>: when a price is re-verified without changes, the page and sitemap dates stay unchanged (full semantics in the <a href="../../pricing-methodology/">pricing methodology</a>). We re-check items individually, but two limitations remain: values the vendor has not published (e.g. some context windows) stay marked undecided rather than invented; and prices change as vendors adjust them — when reusing an example from this article, confirm the current price on the corresponding official page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

